BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Lancaster Libraries": "https://calendar.lancasterlibraries.org/event/lincoln-and-founders-completing-unfinished-work-209662"}
X-SOURCE-ID:lancaster_libraries
SUMMARY:Lincoln and the Founders: Completing the Unfinished Work
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261119T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261119T193000
UID:cc92468bc3645c2a28ee9b4db6aed480@calendar.lancasterlibraries.org
DESCRIPTION:Community\, Presentation\, Teens\, Adults\, Seniors\, Everyone
 \n\nIn the Gettysburg Address\, Abraham Lincoln said that the nation shoul
 d be dedicated "to the unfinished work which they who fought here have thu
 s far so nobly advanced." What was this unfinished work? When Lincoln star
 ted his famous address with "Four score and seven years ago\," he hearkene
 d back to the year 1776 and the Declaration of Independence that stated th
 at "all men are created equal."Much of Lincoln's political ideology stemme
 d from the Declaration of Independence. His words and policies before and 
 during the Civil War demonstrate his reverence for the Declaration. While 
 Lincoln was not an abolitionist\, he did believe that slavery should be pu
 t in "the course of ultimate extinction" by preventing its spread to the t
 erritories.As President\, Lincoln originally worked to preserve the Union 
 that the Founders created. As the Civil War progressed\, he realized that 
 he could no longer restore the Union as it was. He had to restore the Unio
 n so that it was worth saving and to prevent further division over this is
 sue in the future.This program discusses how Lincoln's ideas and policies 
 constantly evolved throughout his political career up to the passage of th
 e 13th Amendment which Lincoln declared "a King's curse" for the issue of 
 slavery in America.
LOCATION:Columbia\, COL Meeting Room
URL:https://calendar.lancasterlibraries.org/event/lincoln-and-founders-com
 pleting-unfinished-work-209662
X-SOURCE:Lancaster Libraries
END:VEVENT
