BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Northmuseum": "https://northmuseum.org/events/7976/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:northmuseum
X-SOURCE:North Museum
DTSTART;TZID=UTC:20261213T200000
DTEND;TZID=UTC:20261213T220000
DTSTAMP:20261113T172203Z
CREATED:20261112T213931Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261113T172203Z
UID:10004724-1797192000-1797199200@northmuseum.org
SUMMARY:Geminids Meteor Shower at Millport Conservancy
DESCRIPTION:[vc_row][vc_column width=”1/1″][vc_column_text uncode_shortcode_id=”128240″] \nGeminids are the most active meteor shower of the year!\nJoin us at Millport Conservancy on the night of Sunday\, December 13th\, to observe what could be the most spectacular meteor shower of the year! Far from the city lights of Lancaster\, the darker skies of Millport mean we could see up to 30+ meteors\, if the weather cooperates! \nThe Geminids are the most active meteor shower of the year\, and are considered one of the two best showers for viewing (along with August’s Perseids). The Geminids are unlike most other meteor showers\, in that they are not likely caused by a debris field from a comet. Instead\, the Geminids have been identified as being spawned from the debris field of 3200 Phaethon\, which is what seems to be an asteroid. Astronomers believe Phaethon is an asteroid because it lacks the trail of a typical comet when it passes the Sun\, and its spectra looks like that of a rocky asteroid. Additionally\, the debris left behind by Phaethon is denser than that of a typical comet. \nHowever\, the object does have a highly elliptical\, or non-circular/”stretched out”\, orbit\, which is atypical for an asteroid but common for comets. Phaethon’s strange orbit gives astronomers pause when labeling it as “just” an asteroid\, and some astronomers have proposed that Phaethon may actually be a “dead comet”\, or even possibly an entirely new type of object that some astronomers are starting to call a “rock comet”. \nWhatever Phaethon is\, the debris field it has left has made for great views here on Earth! Every year\, around December 13th and 14th\, the Earth passes through the debris field. When this happens\, the Earth can be thought of as pushing through the debris\, made up of tiny rocks\, and pulling them into our atmosphere with the help of gravity. All the rocks burning up in our atmosphere is what can be seen as the meteor shower we all know and love. \nKeep an eye out in the coming weeks for more information about the North Museum’s Geminids Star Party\, as well as general observing tips and information about the meteor shower![/vc_column_text][/vc_column][/vc_row]
URL:https://northmuseum.org/events/7976/
LOCATION:Millport Conservancy\, 737 E. Millport Rd.\, Lititz\, PA\, 17543\, United States
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://northmuseum.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/geminids-event-page.jpg
END:VEVENT
